<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper investigates the following optimization problem with a composite objective involving <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> operators:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E1.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\ell(\boldsymbol{w}):=\mathbb{E}_{x\in\mathcal{D}}\Big[\mathcal{L}(x;\boldsymbol{w}):=\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})+\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})\Big]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\ell(\boldsymbol{w}):=\mathbb{E}_{x\in\mathcal{D}}\Big[\mathcal{L}(x;\boldsymbol{w}):=\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})+\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})\Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E1.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s.t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w}):=y_{N},\quad y_{i}=f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i}),\quad\forall i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w}):=y_{N},\quad y_{i}=f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i}),\quad\forall i\in\{1,\ldots,N\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where each operator <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{i-1}}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{i-1}}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th component of the composite function <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The global optimization variable is defined as <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}:=(w_{1}^{\top},w_{2}^{\top},\ldots,w_{N}^{\top})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}:=(w_{1}^{\top},w_{2}^{\top},\ldots,w_{N}^{\top})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to operator <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the total dimension is <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=d_{w_{1}}+d_{w_{2}}+\cdots+d_{w_{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=d_{w_{1}}+d_{w_{2}}+\cdots+d_{w_{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, the term <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a differentiable regularizer used to prevent model overfitting. Quantity <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a data sample drawn from the distribution <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the intermediate output of the <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th operator <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the initial condition <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}=x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}=x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Motivating Examples</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The general formulation (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) arises in various scenarios involving sequential decision-making and operator-wise processing. Several motivating examples are provided below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1: Deep neural network.</span> A deep neural network (DNN) is a prime example of the problem in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), where each operator <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a neural network layer  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Each layer transforms its input <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> using learnable parameters <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce the output <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The composite function <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of these successive transformations, with the final layer generating the value of the loss function. The hierarchical structure and nonlinearity of DNNs allow them to model complex data relationships <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, making them particularly effective for tasks such as image recognition, natural language processing, and reinforcement learning  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2: Finite horizon optimization.</span> Traditional optimization seeks to ensure good performance as the number of iterations <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> approaches infinity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. In contrast, finite horizon optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> is to identify optimal hyperparameter specifically for a small and finite <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, to determine optimal learning rate schedules for gradient descent over <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, we formulate the problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E2.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\{\gamma_{1},\cdots,\gamma_{T}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\{\gamma_{1},\cdots,\gamma_{T}\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\max_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\ \varepsilon(x_{T})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.667em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\max_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\ \varepsilon(x_{T}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E2.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s.t.</span>   </td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad x_{t}=x_{t-1}-\gamma_{t}\nabla g(x_{t-1}),\quad\forall t\in\{1,\ldots,T\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad x_{t}=x_{t-1}-\gamma_{t}\nabla g(x_{t-1}),\quad\forall t\in\{1,\ldots,T\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained through <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> gradient descent steps with learning rate <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at iteration <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Function <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates the performance of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the objective function class. Problem (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Motivating Examples ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) seeks optimal <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve the best possible performance after <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. By setting <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}=\{\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}=\{\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{T+1}=\max_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\varepsilon(x_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{T+1}=\max_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\varepsilon(x_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\{1,\ldots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\{1,\ldots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}=x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}=x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{t}(y_{t-1},\gamma_{t})=y_{t-1}-\gamma_{t}\nabla g(y_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}(y_{t-1},\gamma_{t})=y_{t-1}-\gamma_{t}\nabla g(y_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})=y_{T+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})=y_{T+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, problem (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Motivating Examples ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) reduces to the deterministic version of problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3: Linear quadratic control.</span> The linear quadratic control (LQC) problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> addresses optimal control of linear systems. The goal is to find a control policy based on observed information that minimizes the expected value of a quadratic cost function over a finite time horizon. The problem is given as follows:</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E3.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(x_{t}^{\top}Q_{x}x_{t}+u_{t}^{\top}R_{u}u_{t}\right)+x_{T}^{\top}Q_{T}x_{T}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(x_{t}^{\top}Q_{x}x_{t}+u_{t}^{\top}R_{u}u_{t}\right)+x_{T}^{\top}Q_{T}x_{T}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E3.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s.t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad u_{t}=\pi_{t}(x_{0:t};u_{0:t-1}),\quad\hskip 4.2679pt\forall t\in\{0,\ldots,T-1\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.597em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad u_{t}=\pi_{t}(x_{0:t};u_{0:t-1}),\quad\hskip 4.2679pt\forall t\in\{0,\ldots,T-1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3b)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E3.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad x_{t+1}=Ax_{t}+Bu_{t},\quad\quad\forall t\in\{0,\ldots,T-1\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad x_{t+1}=Ax_{t}+Bu_{t},\quad\quad\forall t\in\{0,\ldots,T-1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3c)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the state <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves according to the linear combination of previous state <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and control input <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with coefficient matrices <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The policy <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> generates control action <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the history of states <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{0:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0:t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Motivating Examples ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) seeks the optimal policy <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=(\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{T-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=(\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{T-1})</annotation></semantics></math> to minimize the total expected cost, determined by weighting matrices <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. By setting <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}=\pi=(\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{T-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}=\pi=(\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{T-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=[u_{t};x_{t+1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=[u_{t};x_{t+1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{t}(y_{t-1},\pi_{t})=[\pi_{t}(x_{0:t};u_{0:t-1});Ax_{t}+Bu_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}(y_{t-1},\pi_{t})=[\pi_{t}(x_{0:t};u_{0:t-1});Ax_{t}+Bu_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>, problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Motivating Examples ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) reduces to (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Stochastic Gradient Descent</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) is a widely used optimization algorithm for solving the problem described in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Given the sequential nature of the operators in this formulation, each iteration of SGD involves both forward and backward passes to compute the stochastic gradient. Let <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}:=\nabla_{w_{i}}\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}:=\nabla_{w_{i}}\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}:=\nabla_{y_{i}}\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}:=\nabla_{y_{i}}\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the stochastic gradients with respect to weight <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and intermediate output <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{N}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{N}=1</annotation></semantics></math> (since the output <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is the final operator’s result). We also use <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}:=\nabla_{w_{i}}\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}:=\nabla_{w_{i}}\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the gradient of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The SGD procedure for optimizing (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is as follows:</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S1.EGx4" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="9"></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E4.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Forward)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{i}^{(t)}=f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{i}^{(t)}=f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad i=1,2,\dots,N," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad i=1,2,\dots,N,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E4.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E4.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Backward)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{i-1}^{(t)}=\nabla_{1}f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}v_{i}^{(t)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{i-1}^{(t)}=\nabla_{1}f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}v_{i}^{(t)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad i=N,N-1,\dots,2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad i=N,N-1,\dots,2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4b)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E4.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E4.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Gradient)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{i}^{(t)}=\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}v_{i}^{(t)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{i}^{(t)}=\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}v_{i}^{(t)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad i=N,N-1,\dots,1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad i=N,N-1,\dots,1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4c)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E4.4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E4.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Update)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{i}^{(t+1)}=w_{i}^{(t)}-\gamma(u_{i}^{(t)}+r_{i}^{(t)})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{i}^{(t+1)}=w_{i}^{(t)}-\gamma(u_{i}^{(t)}+r_{i}^{(t)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad i=N,N-1,\dots,1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad i=N,N-1,\dots,1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4d)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We refer to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intermediate output</span>, <span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intermediate gradient</span>, <span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weight gradient</span>, and <span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weight</span>, respectively. Algorithm (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1.2 Stochastic Gradient Descent ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is structured into four key stages:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forward pass.</span> The input <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is propagated through all <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> operators using (<a href="#S1.E4.1" title="In 4 ‣ 1.2 Stochastic Gradient Descent ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4a</span></a>), where <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}^{(t)}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}^{(t)}=x</annotation></semantics></math>. This step sequentially evaluates all operators.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Backward pass.</span> Gradient information is propagated in reverse order. Intermediate gradient <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed at each operator <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> starting from <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=N</annotation></semantics></math> down to <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i-1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i-1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated by applying the chain rule (<a href="#S1.E4.2" title="In 4 ‣ 1.2 Stochastic Gradient Descent ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4b</span></a>), where <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{1}f_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{1}f_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian of the operator <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient.</span> The parameter gradients <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> are computed at each operator by combining <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{2}f_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{w_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{2}f_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{w_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the local Jacobians of the operators with respect to the weight <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, see the update in (<a href="#S1.E4.3" title="In 4 ‣ 1.2 Stochastic Gradient Descent ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4c</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weight update.</span> The weights <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated with (<a href="#S1.E4.4" title="In 4 ‣ 1.2 Stochastic Gradient Descent ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4d</span></a>), where <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the learning rate, and the update proceeds from <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=N</annotation></semantics></math> down to <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This iterative procedure minimizes the composite objective function <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> together with the regularizer <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> by updating the operator-wise parameters <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> using gradient information with respect to both intermediate outputs and model weights. Note that <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, so its gradient <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=\nabla_{w_{i}}\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=\nabla_{w_{i}}\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed and added directly in the update step, without participating in the forward/backward passes for evaluating <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practical implementations, gradient evaluation is susceptible to perturbations in both the forward and backward passes. To model these effects, we introduce forward and backward perturbation terms, <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\delta_{i}^{(t)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\delta_{i}^{(t)}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, for the <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th operator at iteration <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. We model perturbations only in the forward/backward evaluation of the sample-dependent composite loss <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(x;\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math>; the parameter-only regularizer <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as exact and contributes deterministically via <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=\nabla_{w_{i}}\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=\nabla_{w_{i}}\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math>. Incorporating these perturbations leads to an SGD update with perturbed forward and backward passes:</p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S1.EGx5" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="9"></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E5.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Forward)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}=f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})+{\delta_{i}^{(t)}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}=f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})+{\delta_{i}^{(t)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i=1,2,\cdots,N," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i=1,2,\cdots,N,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E5.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E5.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Backward)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{v}_{i-1}^{(t)}=\nabla_{1}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}+{\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{v}_{i-1}^{(t)}=\nabla_{1}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}+{\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i=N,N-1,\cdots,2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i=N,N-1,\cdots,2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5b)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E5.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E5.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Gradient)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{u}_{i}^{(t)}=\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{u}_{i}^{(t)}=\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i=N,N-1,\cdots,1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i=N,N-1,\cdots,1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5c)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E5.4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E5.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Update)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{i}^{(t+1)}=w_{i}^{(t)}-\gamma(\tilde{u}_{i}^{(t)}+r_{i}^{(t)})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{i}^{(t+1)}=w_{i}^{(t)}-\gamma(\tilde{u}_{i}^{(t)}+r_{i}^{(t)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i=N,N-1,\dots,1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i=N,N-1,\dots,1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5d)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corrupted intermediate output and intermediate gradient, induced by (random) perturbations <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Since the weight gradient <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the corrupted intermediate gradient <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E5.3" title="In 5 ‣ 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5c</span></a>), it yields a corrupted gradient <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates algorithm procedure listed in (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20646v1/perturbed_sgd.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:438/169;" width="438" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An illustration of SGD algorithm with perturbed forward and backward passes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbed SGD framework (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) naturally emerges in numerous practical scenarios. For instance, mixed-precision training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, widely adopted for DNN, combines low-precision computations (FP16/INT8) with FP32 model weights to reduce memory consumption and accelerate training on specialized hardware. This precision reduction inherently introduces systematic perturbations: forward passes in lower precision generate rounding errors (<math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), while reduced-precision intermediate gradient computations yield backward perturbations (<math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). Similarly, pipeline-parallel DNN training partitions neural network layers across multiple devices, requiring communication of intermediate outputs and gradients. To mitigate communication overhead, these variables undergo compression before transmission <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, inducing forward and backward perturbations at operator boundaries; see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Non-convergence With Top-K Compressor ‣ Appendix A Counter examples ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> for a simple two-layer example where a deterministic Top-1 compressor prevents convergence even though the uncompressed method converges. Additionally, hardware-induced errors—such as bit flips from cosmic rays or voltage instability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>—introduce unpredictable computational perturbations during forward and backward passes.
Furthermore, in the LQC problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Motivating Examples ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), the state <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> may be affected by random Gaussian noise, e.g., <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+1}=Ax_{t}+Bu_{t}+\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+1}=Ax_{t}+Bu_{t}+\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma_{\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma_{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math>. These diverse sources of perturbations demonstrate that SGD with perturbed forward and backward passes captures the fundamental dynamics of modern optimization, where exact gradients may not always be feasible.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4 </span>Open Questions, Theoretical Challenges, and Contributions</h3>

<section id="S1.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.4.1 </span>Open questions</h4>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the widespread use of SGD with perturbed forward and backward passes in modern optimization and machine learning, our theoretical understanding remains limited. Most existing literature focuses exclusively on noise or perturbations to weight gradients <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E5.3" title="In 5 ‣ 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5c</span></a>), where errors remain localized—they neither propagate forward through subsequent operators nor backward through intermediate gradients, affecting only the model weight update. In contrast, perturbations to intermediate outputs <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E5.1" title="In 5 ‣ 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5a</span></a>) and intermediate gradients <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E5.2" title="In 5 ‣ 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5b</span></a>) cascade through the computational graph, accumulating across operators to create compounded errors that fundamentally complicate convergence analysis. This paper investigates the following three fundamental open questions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I2.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span>.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Error dynamics)</span> How can the propagation and accumulation of compounded errors through the forward and backward passes be theoretically characterized?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I2.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Convergence)</span> Can SGD converge to optimal solutions under perturbed forward and backward passes, and how do these perturbations quantitatively impact the convergence behavior?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I2.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Robustness)</span> Under what conditions do perturbations not affect convergence rates, and what properties of perturbations contribute to this robustness?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.4.2 </span>Theoretical challenges</h4>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main challenge arises from compounded error propagation through computational graphs. Standard SGD analyses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> treat gradient errors as additive noise that is independent of computation—modeling perturbed gradients as <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}=\nabla f(w)+\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}=\nabla f(w)+\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with exogenous <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, framework (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) generates perturbations endogenously that cascade through operators, creating complex inter-operator dependencies. Consider a linear neural network with <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(y_{i-1},W_{i})=W_{i}y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(y_{i-1},W_{i})=W_{i}y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, zero forward perturbations (<math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}=0</annotation></semantics></math>), and constant backward perturbations (<math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}=\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}=\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). The weight gradient <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> under both models reveals the distinction:</p>
<table id="S1.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S1.EGx6" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="7"></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E6.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E6.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Add. perturbation)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E6.1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{u}_{1}=W_{2}^{\top}\cdots W_{N}^{\top}v_{N}\cdot v_{1}^{\top}+\varepsilon," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{u}_{1}=W_{2}^{\top}\cdots W_{N}^{\top}v_{N}\cdot v_{1}^{\top}+\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E6.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.E6.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Acc. perturbation)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E6.2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{u}_{1}=\left(W_{2}^{\top}\left(\cdots\left(W_{N}^{\top}v_{N}+\varepsilon_{N}\right)+\cdots\right)+\varepsilon_{2}\right)v_{1}^{\top}+\varepsilon_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{u}_{1}=\left(W_{2}^{\top}\left(\cdots\left(W_{N}^{\top}v_{N}+\varepsilon_{N}\right)+\cdots\right)+\varepsilon_{2}\right)v_{1}^{\top}+\varepsilon_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the perturbed gradient with respect to <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, while “Add.” and “Acc.” denote additive and accumulative perturbations, respectively. The perturbed formulation (<a href="#S1.E6.2" title="In 6 ‣ 1.4.2 Theoretical challenges ‣ 1.4 Open Questions, Theoretical Challenges, and Contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6b</span></a>) exhibits three pathological behaviors absent from standard SGD analyses with additive noise: (1) <span id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geometric error amplification.</span> The deepest perturbation <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied by the matrix product <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{1}^{\top}\cdots W_{N-1}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}^{\top}\cdots W_{N-1}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, potentially causing exponential growth with network depth—a phenomenon that can trigger convergence failure. (2) <span id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cascading error interference.</span> The nested structure creates nonlinear interactions between operator-wise perturbations, where errors compound in ways that simple noise models cannot capture.
(3) <span id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Biased gradient estimates.</span> Even when perturbations <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> have zero expectation, the nonlinear operators in (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) induce bias such that <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})]\neq\nabla_{2}f_{i}({y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})]\neq\nabla_{2}f_{i}({y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math>.
These effects critically challenge analysis, requiring new theoretical frameworks beyond classical SGD theory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.4.3 </span>Contributions</h4>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper provides the first comprehensive analysis for SGD under perturbed forward and backward passes. Our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I3.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C1.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We theoretically characterize the propagation of forward and backward perturbations within a single gradient step, demonstrating that these perturbations amplify exponentially with the number of operators, which addresses Q1.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I3.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C2.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish convergence guarantees for perturbed SGD on both general non-convex functions and those satisfying the Polyak–Łojasiewicz (PL) condition. By explicitly quantifying the impact of forward and backward errors on the convergence rate, we address open question Q2.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I3.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C3.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I3.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify the specific conditions under which forward and backward perturbations do not deteriorate the asymptotic order of the convergence rate, directly addressing open question Q3.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a side result, our analysis provides an explanation for the “gradient spiking” phenomenon commonly observed in deep learning. These spikes are sudden, temporary jumps in the gradient, often triggered by perturbations during the forward and backward passes. While some spikes are recoverable, meaning they can be corrected during the training process, others can lead to training divergence, see Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1.4.3 Contributions ‣ 1.4 Open Questions, Theoretical Challenges, and Contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for the illustration. Our theoretical analysis of the perturbed SGD (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) clearly establishes the conditions under which the optimization can recover or experience a complete crash.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/spike_phenomenon.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:438/157;" width="438" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Comparison of gradient spike patterns leading to contrasting outcomes. The gradient norm (left) and loss (right) trajectories illustrate two scenarios: a large-magnitude spike (orange) that permits rapid recovery and convergence, versus a more moderate spike (blue) that triggers persistent deviation and eventual non-convergence.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization.</span> This paper is organized as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Works ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents related work. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents preliminaries, including notations and assumptions. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> analyzes the propagation of forward and backward computation errors. Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> derives the convergence rate of the SGD algorithm with computation error under non-smooth settings and PL conditions. Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> establishes conditions on computation error that guarantee convergence and enable recovery from gradient spikes. Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents numerical experiments validating our theoretical findings. Finally, Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Conclusion and Future Works ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> concludes the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis builds upon and extends several lines of theoretical work on optimization algorithms subject to computational inaccuracies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithms with inexact gradient oracles.</span> The effect of computational inaccuracy on gradient methods has been studied extensively under inexact first-order oracle models, in which the algorithm accesses a perturbed gradient at each iteration. Classical results analyze gradient descent with deterministic errors (e.g., finite differences in zeroth-order methods, early termination of inner solvers, discretization errors in PDE-constrained optimization, and surrogate model approximations) and derive convergence and complexity guarantees that degrade gracefully with the error level <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. In stochastic settings, the gradient is typically corrupted by additive sampling noise from random data sampling; related analyses quantify the convergence performance typically under conditional moment or bounded-variance assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. A parallel line of work examines inexact proximal point and proximal-gradient methods, in which proximal subproblems are solved approximately while still ensuring descent and convergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. More recent developments refine oracle models for composite structures and higher-order inexactness, and establish non-asymptotic bounds that explicitly track oracle accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Many pre-training approaches for large language models can be viewed as first-order methods with inexact gradients due to communication or computation errors. Examples include gradient quantization and sparsification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, mixed-precision training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, and gradient clipping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. The analysis typically treats the gradient as a perturbation of the ideal stochastic gradient and relates convergence to error statistics (bias, variance, or relative bounds). This perspective is particularly powerful when the perturbation can be modeled as an <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">additive error</em> to the computed gradient, as illustrated in (<a href="#S1.E6.1" title="In 6 ‣ 1.4.2 Theoretical challenges ‣ 1.4 Open Questions, Theoretical Challenges, and Contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6a</span></a>), enabling standard descent and martingale analyses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbation mechanism in (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) differs fundamentally from standard inexact-oracle models. Perturbations are injected <em id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within</em> the forward–backward pass at intermediate activations and adjoints, propagating through the computational graph before the gradient forms. The resulting error is endogenous, shaped by local Jacobians at the current iterate, creating inter-operator dependencies. Moreover, even zero-mean perturbations can induce gradient bias due to the composite structure; see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Zero-mean Noise can Lead to Biased Evaluated Gradient ‣ Appendix A Counter examples ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> for a simple scalar example. These considerations motivate our analysis of SGD with perturbed forward–backward passes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stochastic compositional optimization (SCO).</span>
Stochastic compositional optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> studies problems with nested expectation structure, such as</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{w\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}F(w):=\mathbb{E}_{\zeta}\bigl[f_{\zeta}\bigl(G(w)\bigr)\bigr],\qquad G(w):=\mathbb{E}_{\xi}[g_{\xi}(w)]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{w\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}F(w):=\mathbb{E}_{\zeta}\bigl[f_{\zeta}\bigl(G(w)\bigr)\bigr],\qquad G(w):=\mathbb{E}_{\xi}[g_{\xi}(w)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and their multi-level generalizations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> (see also constrained or projection-free variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>). A key difficulty is that computing <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F(w)</annotation></semantics></math> requires evaluating the derivative at <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(w)</annotation></semantics></math>, an inner expectation that is not observable from a single sample <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. Practical methods thus maintain auxiliary variables (typically one per level) to track the nested expectations and form quasi-gradients by combining component derivative estimates with these tracked quantities. Since the outer derivative employs an evolving approximation instead of the exact inner expectations, the descent directions become biased. Consequently, the analysis focuses on controlling the coupled iterate-tracking dynamics through multi-timescale step sizes or single-loop tracking/variance-reduction designs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our setting differs from SCO in both formulation and error mechanism. At a high level, both SCO and our framework involve compositional graphs where intermediate quantities are accessed inexactly, leading to biased gradient surrogates. However, the source and nature of inexactness are fundamentally different. In SCO, inexactness arises from <em id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">estimating inner expectations</em> within each operator; this bias can be reduced algorithmically by allocating more computational effort to inner estimation or tracking—for example, using larger inner batches or more accurate variance-reduced estimators. In contrast, our setting (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) involves a single expectation, where inexactness stems from perturbations <em id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within</em> the forward and backward passes themselves. Crucially, increasing batch size reduces sampling variance but <em id="S2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">does not</em> diminish these within-pass perturbations, which persist independently of sample size. Moreover, perturbations in our setting are depth-coupled: they propagate and accumulate along sample paths through the computational graph. This central challenge—depth-wise propagation and accumulation of intermediate disturbances—lies beyond the scope of existing SCO analyses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AQ-SGD. </span>The most closely related work is AQ-SGD, which analyzes activation quantization for distributed pipeline training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>. Critically, AQ-SGD is tailored to pipeline parallelism with communication-induced perturbations and does not address general forward-backward perturbation mechanisms or provide the depth-wise error propagation and disturbance-frequency analysis developed here.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces necessary notations and assumptions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Notations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the gradient of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,N</annotation></semantics></math>, the partial derivative of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to parameter <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{i}\ell(\boldsymbol{w}):=\frac{\partial\ell(\boldsymbol{w})}{\partial w_{i}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{i}\ell(\boldsymbol{w}):=\frac{\partial\ell(\boldsymbol{w})}{\partial w_{i}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each operator <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i}):\mathbb{R}^{d_{i-1}}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i}):\mathbb{R}^{d_{i-1}}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Jacobian matrices with respect to the input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and parameter <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{1}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{1}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{w_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{w_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We also denote <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}_{21}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{w_{i}}\times d_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mn>21</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}_{21}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{w_{i}}\times d_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the partial derivative of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the Euclidean norm for vectors and Frobenius norm for matrices and tensors, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\text{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>op</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\text{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the induced operator norm. Throughout the paper, superscripts <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> indicate the iteration index, while subscripts <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> denote the component index. For any family of component-wise vectors <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and their perturbed counterparts), we use boldface <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}:=(a_{1}^{\top},\ldots,a_{N}^{\top})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}:=(a_{1}^{\top},\ldots,a_{N}^{\top})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the stacked/concatenated vector across components
(and similarly <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the perturbed version). We define the minimum objective value as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{*}:=\min_{\boldsymbol{w}}\ell(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>𝒘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{*}:=\min_{\boldsymbol{w}}\ell(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> and the initial optimality gap as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{0}:=\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(0)})-\ell^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{0}:=\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(0)})-\ell^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The notation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\lesssim b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≲</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\lesssim b</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq Cb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq Cb</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> independent of problem parameters. Notation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> hides logarithmic dependence on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the stochastic setting, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the filtration generated up to iteration <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{t}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{t}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the conditional expectation given <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{i}^{(t)}=\sigma(\mathcal{F}^{(t)}\cup\{\tilde{y}_{1}^{(t)},\ldots,\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{i}^{(t)}=\sigma(\mathcal{F}^{(t)}\cup\{\tilde{y}_{1}^{(t)},\ldots,\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}\})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the filtration generated by perturbed outputs up to operator <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in the forward pass, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{i}^{(t)}=\sigma(\mathcal{G}_{N}^{(t)}\cup\{\tilde{v}_{N}^{(t)},\ldots,\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{i}^{(t)}=\sigma(\mathcal{G}_{N}^{(t)}\cup\{\tilde{v}_{N}^{(t)},\ldots,\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}\})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the filtration generated by the full forward pass and backward gradients from operator <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> down to operator <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote conditional expectations with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Third-Order Tensor and Its Operator Norm</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define multiplication between third-order tensors and matrices, along with the induced operator norm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}:=(\mathcal{H}_{j\alpha\beta})\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}:=(\mathcal{H}_{j\alpha\beta})\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a third-order tensor and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=(A_{\alpha\beta})\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=(A_{\alpha\beta})\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a matrix. The product <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(A)\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(A)\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by contracting over the last two indices:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\mathcal{H}(A)]_{j}:=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{m}\sum_{\beta=1}^{n}\mathcal{H}_{j\alpha\beta}A_{\alpha\beta},\qquad j=1,\dots,p." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}(A)]_{j}:=\sum_{\alpha=1}^{m}\sum_{\beta=1}^{n}\mathcal{H}_{j\alpha\beta}A_{\alpha\beta},\qquad j=1,\dots,p.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">That is, for each <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the component <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}(A)]_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}(A)]_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\alpha,\beta}\mathcal{H}_{j\alpha\beta}A_{\alpha\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\alpha,\beta}\mathcal{H}_{j\alpha\beta}A_{\alpha\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. From (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.2 Third-Order Tensor and Its Operator Norm ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), we define the operator norm of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathcal{H}\|_{\mathrm{op}}:=\sup_{A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n},\,A\neq 0}\frac{\|\mathcal{H}(A)\|_{2}}{\|A\|}=\sup_{\|A\|=1}\|\mathcal{H}(A)\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathcal{H}\|_{\mathrm{op}}:=\sup_{A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n},\,A\neq 0}\frac{\|\mathcal{H}(A)\|_{2}}{\|A\|}=\sup_{\|A\|=1}\|\mathcal{H}(A)\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathcal{H}\|_{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathcal{H}\|_{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> measures the largest amplification of the output norm <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathcal{H}(A)\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathcal{H}(A)\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> when the input matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> has unit Frobenius norm. The Frobenius norm of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathcal{H}\|:=\Big(\sum_{j,\alpha,\beta}\mathcal{H}_{j,\alpha,\beta}^{2}\Big)^{\frac{1}{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathcal{H}\|:=\Big(\sum_{j,\alpha,\beta}\mathcal{H}_{j,\alpha,\beta}^{2}\Big)^{\frac{1}{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Assumptions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following two assumptions are standard in the convergence analysis of stochastic gradient algorithms.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Smoothness of the loss function)</span><span id="Thmassumption1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a constant <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\nabla\ell}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\nabla\ell}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\nabla\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\nabla\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmassumption2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Stochasticity)</span><span id="Thmassumption2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a constant <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any given <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the unperturbed gradient oracles satisfy:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\in\mathcal{D}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{w}}\mathcal{L}(x;\boldsymbol{w})]=\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w});\quad\mathbb{E}_{x\in\mathcal{D}}\left[\left\|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{w}}\mathcal{L}(x;\boldsymbol{w})-\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w})\right\|^{2}\right]\leq\sigma^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\in\mathcal{D}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{w}}\mathcal{L}(x;\boldsymbol{w})]=\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w});\quad\mathbb{E}_{x\in\mathcal{D}}\left[\left\|\nabla_{\boldsymbol{w}}\mathcal{L}(x;\boldsymbol{w})-\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w})\right\|^{2}\right]\leq\sigma^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also make the following assumption on the smoothness of each operator.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmassumption3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Operator smoothness)</span><span id="Thmassumption3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,N</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist constants <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\nabla f},C_{\nabla^{2}f},L_{f},L_{\nabla f},L_{\nabla^{2}f}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\nabla f},C_{\nabla^{2}f},L_{f},L_{\nabla f},L_{\nabla^{2}f}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{w_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Jacobians satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{j}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\|_{\text{op}}\leq C_{\nabla f}\quad(j=1,2),\qquad\|\nabla^{2}_{21}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\|_{\text{op}}\leq C_{\nabla^{2}f}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">op</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>21</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">op</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{j}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\|_{\text{op}}\leq C_{\nabla f}\quad(j=1,2),\qquad\|\nabla^{2}_{21}f_{i}(y_{i-1},w_{i})\|_{\text{op}}\leq C_{\nabla^{2}f}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The operator </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is Lipschitz continuous with respect to </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with constant </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Here, Lipschitz continuity of </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is measured in the Euclidean norm.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The mappings </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are Lipschitz continuous with respect to both </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, with constants </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\nabla f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\nabla f}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\nabla^{2}f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\nabla^{2}f}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, respectively. Here, Lipschitz continuity of </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is measured in the Frobenius norm.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is an operator-regularity condition that controls how perturbations amplify through the computational graph during the forward and backward passes. Bounded-Jacobian and Lipschitz-type conditions are standard in analyses involving intermediate perturbations; see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><sup id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup></span>
              
              
              
            When the component mappings are twice differentiable, the Lipschitz requirements on the derivative maps in Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> can be interpreted as bounded variation of the local Jacobians, or equivalently, bounded mixed second-order derivatives.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is <em id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> an algorithmic constraint: throughout the paper we analyze the plain SGD update in (<a href="#S1.E5.4" title="In 5 ‣ 1.3 SGD with Perturbed Forward and Backward Passes ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5d</span></a>) without projection or clipping. That said, Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> can be interpreted <em id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally</em>: it suffices for the stated regularity bounds to hold on a compact region containing the iterates and intermediate states visited during training. Such compactness can arise, for example, from finite data combined with bounded parameter regimes, explicit projection to a compact set, or standard clipping/normalization practices. Our goal is to isolate the effect of forward/backward perturbations; incorporating such safeguards would only strengthen stability in practice.
Finally, we make the following assumption of the Polyak-Łojasiewicz (PL) condition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> for <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmassumption4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(PL condition)<span id="Thmassumption4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist a constant <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w})\right\|^{2}\geq 2\mu(\ell(\boldsymbol{w})-\ell^{*})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w})\right\|^{2}\geq 2\mu(\ell(\boldsymbol{w})-\ell^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The PL condition can be implied by strong convexity. In particular, adding an <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularizer <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})=\frac{\lambda}{2}\|\boldsymbol{w}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{w})=\frac{\lambda}{2}\|\boldsymbol{w}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> makes the loss <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\boldsymbol{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\boldsymbol{w})</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex whenever the data-fitting term is convex, which in turn ensures the PL inequality holds with <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Error Propagation Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section characterizes the propagation of forward and backward perturbations within a single gradient step. We begin with the following useful lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, there exists a constant <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}^{2}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}^{2}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|v_{i}^{(t)}\|^{2}\leq C_{v}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|v_{i}^{(t)}\|^{2}\leq C_{v}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>, the vector <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\nabla_{1}f_{N}(y_{N-1}^{(t)},w_{N}^{(t)})\right)^{\top}\cdots\left(\nabla_{1}f_{i+2}(y_{i+1}^{(t)},w_{i+2}^{(t)})\right)^{\top}\cdot\left(\nabla_{1}f_{i+1}(y_{i}^{(t)},w_{i+1}^{(t)})\right)^{\top}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\nabla_{1}f_{N}(y_{N-1}^{(t)},w_{N}^{(t)})\right)^{\top}\cdots\left(\nabla_{1}f_{i+2}(y_{i+1}^{(t)},w_{i+2}^{(t)})\right)^{\top}\cdot\left(\nabla_{1}f_{i+1}(y_{i}^{(t)},w_{i+1}^{(t)})\right)^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">This yields the upper bound</span></p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\prod_{k=i+1}^{N}\left\|\nabla_{1}f_{k}(y_{k-1}^{(t)},w_{k}^{(t)})\right\|_{\text{op}}^{2}\leq C_{\nabla f}^{2(N-i)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mtext>op</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\prod_{k=i+1}^{N}\left\|\nabla_{1}f_{k}(y_{k-1}^{(t)},w_{k}^{(t)})\right\|_{\text{op}}^{2}\leq C_{\nabla f}^{2(N-i)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the first inequality holds because <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{1}f_{N}(y_{N-1}^{(t)},w_{N}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{1}f_{N}(y_{N-1}^{(t)},w_{N}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so its operator norm equals its Euclidean norm. Since <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed constant and <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{N}^{(t)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{N}^{(t)}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, taking <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}=\max\{1,C_{\nabla f}^{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}=\max\{1,C_{\nabla f}^{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|v_{i}^{(t)}\|^{2}\leq C_{v}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|v_{i}^{(t)}\|^{2}\leq C_{v}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> ensures that the intermediate backward gradients <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> remain uniformly bounded across all operators and iterations. This controls how perturbations introduced during the backward pass are amplified as they propagate toward earlier operators. The bound <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}=\max\{1,C_{\nabla f}^{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}=\max\{1,C_{\nabla f}^{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects worst-case accumulation of Jacobian norms across the network depth.
The following theorem presents an upper bound of the <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-error term:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> holds, then for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\cdots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\cdots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist constants <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{e}_{\delta_{i}},C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{e}_{\delta_{i}},C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, which are defined in (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In Proof. ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>), such that:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\big[\|\tilde{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}-{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}\|^{2}\big]\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\big[\|\tilde{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}-{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}\|^{2}\big]\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}C^{e}_{\delta_{i}}\mathbb{E}\big[\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\|^{2}\big]+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}\mathbb{E}\big[\|\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\|^{2}\big]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}C^{e}_{\delta_{i}}\mathbb{E}\big[\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\|^{2}\big]+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}\mathbb{E}\big[\|\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\|^{2}\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">holds for all <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\cdots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\cdots,T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.8.1" class="ltx_text">We first consider the gradient evaluation at the last component. We have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E14X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{u}_{N}^{(t)}-{u}_{N}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla_{2}f_{N}(\tilde{y}_{N-1}^{(t)},w_{N}^{(t)})-\nabla_{2}f_{N}(y_{N-1}^{(t)},w_{N}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{u}_{N}^{(t)}-{u}_{N}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla_{2}f_{N}(\tilde{y}_{N-1}^{(t)},w_{N}^{(t)})-\nabla_{2}f_{N}(y_{N-1}^{(t)},w_{N}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E14Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{N-1}^{(t)}-y_{N-1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\leq 2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla^{2}f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{N-1}^{(t)}-y_{N-1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{N-1}^{(t)}-y_{N-1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\leq 2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla^{2}f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{N-1}^{(t)}-y_{N-1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.9.1" class="ltx_text">where the inequality follows from the smoothness of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{2}f_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{2}f_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and the fact that <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}^{2}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}^{2}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E15X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{u}_{i}^{(t)}-{u}_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{u}_{i}^{(t)}-{u}_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})^{\top}v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E15Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})\right\|_{\text{op}}^{2}\left\|\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mtext>op</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})\right\|_{\text{op}}^{2}\left\|\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E15Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+2\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})-\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})\right\|_{\text{op}}^{2}\left\|v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mtext>op</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+2\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla_{2}f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})-\nabla_{2}f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})\right\|_{\text{op}}^{2}\left\|v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E15Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2C_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}-y_{i-1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2C_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}-y_{i-1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.10.1" class="ltx_text">where the second inequality follows from the smoothness of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{2}f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{2}f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> together with the boundedness of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{2}f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{2}f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Summing over <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E16X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}-{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{u}_{i}^{(t)}-{u}_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}-{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{u}_{i}^{(t)}-{u}_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E16Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2C_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-y_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2C_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-v_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-y_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.11.1" class="ltx_text">In view of (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Proof. ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), we next analyze the error between <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E17X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-{v}_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\hskip-1.42262pt\right]\hskip-2.84526pt=\hskip-2.84526pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\nabla_{1}f_{i+1}(\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)},w_{i+1}^{(t)})^{\top}\tilde{v}_{i+1}^{(t)}\hskip-1.42262pt-\hskip-1.42262pt\nabla_{1}f_{i+1}(y_{i}^{(t)},w_{i+1}^{(t)})^{\top}v_{i+1}^{(t)}\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\hskip-1.42262pt\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\tilde{v}_{i}^{(t)}-{v}_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\hskip-1.42262pt\right]\hskip-2.84526pt=\hskip-2.84526pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\nabla_{1}f_{i+1}(\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)},w_{i+1}^{(t)})^{\top}\tilde{v}_{i+1}^{(t)}\hskip-1.42262pt-\hskip-1.42262pt\nabla_{1}f_{i+1}(y_{i}^{(t)},w_{i+1}^{(t)})^{\top}v_{i+1}^{(t)}\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\hskip-1.42262pt\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E17Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 3C_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{v}_{i+1}^{(t)}-v_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+3C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-y_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+3\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 3C_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{v}_{i+1}^{(t)}-v_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+3C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-y_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+3\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E17Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cdots\leq 3C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j-i}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{j}^{(t)}-y_{j}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+3\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j-i}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{j+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cdots\leq 3C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j-i}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{j}^{(t)}-y_{j}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+3\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j-i}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{j+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.12.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we bound the error between <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E18X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-{y}_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})-f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})+\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-{y}_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})-f_{i}(y_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})+\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E18Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2L_{f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}-{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+2\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\leq\cdots\leq 2\sum_{j=1}^{i}(2L_{f}^{2})^{i-j}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{j}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2L_{f}^{2}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}-{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+2\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\leq\cdots\leq 2\sum_{j=1}^{i}(2L_{f}^{2})^{i-j}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{j}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.13.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting (<a href="#S4.E17" title="In Proof. ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) into (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Proof. ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E19X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}-{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}-{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E19Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 6C_{\nabla f}^{2}C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j-i}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{j}^{(t)}-y_{j}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 6C_{\nabla f}^{2}C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j-i}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{j}^{(t)}-y_{j}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E19Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-y_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+6C_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j-i}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{j+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-y_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+6C_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j-i}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{j+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E19Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{j=0}^{i}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j}\right]\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-y_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+2\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{j=1}^{i}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j}\right]\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{j=0}^{i}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j}\right]\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{y}_{i}^{(t)}-y_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+2\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{j=1}^{i}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j}\right]\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.14.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In Proof. ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) into (<a href="#S4.E19" title="In Proof. ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) then gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.E20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E20X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}-{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\tilde{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}-{{\boldsymbol{u}}}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 4C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left(\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{k=0}^{j}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{k}\right](2L_{f}^{2})^{j-i}\right)\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 4C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left(\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{k=0}^{j}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{k}\right](2L_{f}^{2})^{j-i}\right)\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E20Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{j=1}^{i}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j}\right]\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>]</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{j=1}^{i}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j}\right]\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.15" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.15.1" class="ltx_text">Defining</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C^{e}_{\delta_{i}}:=4C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{k=0}^{j}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{k}\right](2L_{f}^{2})^{j-i},\quad C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}:=\sum_{j=1}^{i}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C^{e}_{\delta_{i}}:=4C_{v}^{2}L_{\nabla f}^{2}\sum_{j=i}^{N-1}\left[\sum_{k=0}^{j}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{k}\right](2L_{f}^{2})^{j-i},\quad C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}:=\sum_{j=1}^{i}(3C_{\nabla f}^{2})^{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.16" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.16.1" class="ltx_text">we conclude that (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) holds for all <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Inequality (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) shows that the <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> error in the stochastic gradient due to forward and backward perturbations is controlled by the <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> magnitudes of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Crucially, the coefficients <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{e}_{\delta_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{e}_{\delta_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> scale exponentially with the depth of the computational graph, reflecting <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\mathcal{O}(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\mathcal{O}(N)}</annotation></semantics></math> error amplification. This exponential dependence illustrates how small perturbations at individual operators can compound into significant gradient distortions as they propagate through the network.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We next bound the second moment of the bias with the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, it holds for <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\cdots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\cdots,N</annotation></semantics></math> that:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E22X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
