<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Setting <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the standard SGD without perturbations in forward and backward passes. In this case, choosing the step size <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-1}\ln T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-1}\ln T)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E38" title="In Theorem 5. ‣ 5.2 Convergence with PL Condition ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) yields the classical <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> convergence rate of SGD under the PL condition.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Interpreting the Convergence Theorems</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 5.2 Convergence with PL Condition ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> offer a clean separation among optimization progress, stochastic sampling noise, and endogenous perturbation effects induced by corrupted forward/backward passes, providing a structured framework for examining the distinct role of each component.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exponential coefficient scaling with depth.</span>
The coefficients presented in Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 5.2 Convergence with PL Condition ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, namely <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{e}_{\delta_{i}},C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i}},C^{b}_{\delta_{i}},C^{b}_{\varepsilon_{i}},\widetilde{C}^{b}_{\delta_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{e}_{\delta_{i}},C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i}},C^{b}_{\delta_{i}},C^{b}_{\varepsilon_{i}},\widetilde{C}^{b}_{\delta_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, encode how perturbations at individual operators impact the gradient error. As the explicit formulas in Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reveal, these coefficients take a geometric-series form and can therefore scale <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exponentially</em> in the depth <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> whenever per-operator Lipschitz factors exceed <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structural asymmetry between forward and backward perturbations.</span>
No single scalar ordering holds across all regimes, as the coefficients are operator- and depth-dependent. Nevertheless, Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 5.2 Convergence with PL Condition ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> imply a robust qualitative hierarchy: <em id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forward-pass perturbations <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> can introduce non-vanishing bias through higher-order effects even when zero-mean, whereas backward-pass perturbations <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> do not.</em> This asymmetry is the primary structural reason why Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> permits much weaker constraints on <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> than on <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (see further discussion in Sections <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> and <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitivity to perturbation location.</span>
Forward perturbations near the input (i.e., operators with small index <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>) propagate through many subsequent operators, whereas backward perturbations near the output (i.e., operators with large index <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>) propagate through many upstream Jacobian factors.
Consequently, when only a single layer is perturbed, the two cases exhibit opposite depth hierarchies: for <em id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forward</em> perturbations <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{1},\delta_{N-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{1},\delta_{N-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, the shallow error <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically far more harmful than the deep error <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>; conversely, for <em id="S5.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backward</em> perturbations <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon_{N},\varepsilon_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon_{N},\varepsilon_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the deep error <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically far more harmful than its shallow counterpart <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This hierarchy is captured by the layer-dependent coefficients <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{e}_{\delta_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{e}_{\delta_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{e}_{\varepsilon_{i+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 5.2 Convergence with PL Condition ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section identifies conditions on the magnitude and frequency of perturbations under which SGD still converges. We focus on two types of perturbations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S6.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span></span> 
<div id="S6.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frequent perturbations:</span> Perturbations occur at every iteration with unknown energy, as in communication errors in pipeline compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>. We seek to characterize the energy levels that still guarantee convergence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S6.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span></span> 
<div id="S6.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intermittent perturbation: </span> Perturbations occur sporadically with fixed energy, as in bit flips. We seek to characterize the frequency of occurrence that still guarantees convergence.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following corollary provides sufficient conditions on the energy of frequent perturbations that guarantee convergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Smoothness of the loss function). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, SGD achieves a convergence rate of <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> if for all <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>, the perturbations <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{8}},\quad\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{4}},\quad\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|\lesssim 1,\quad\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{4}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{8}},\quad\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{4}},\quad\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|\lesssim 1,\quad\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{4}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 (PL condition). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> also holds, then SGD achieves a convergence rate of <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> if for all <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>, the perturbations satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S6.E44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.E44X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E44X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E44X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{4}}\ln^{\frac{1}{4}}T,\quad\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{2}}\ln^{\frac{1}{2}}T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≲</mo><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mi>ln</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></msup><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>‖</mo><mo>≲</mo><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mi>ln</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{4}}\ln^{\frac{1}{4}}T,\quad\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{2}}\ln^{\frac{1}{2}}T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E44Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E44Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E44Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim 1,\hskip 49.79231pt\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{2}}\ln^{\frac{1}{2}}T." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="5.147em">,</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>‖</mo><mo>≲</mo><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mi>ln</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim 1,\hskip 49.79231pt\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{2}}\ln^{\frac{1}{2}}T.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Setting <math id="Thmcorollary1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> and substituting (<a href="#S6.E43" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a>) into (<a href="#S5.E37" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>) yields</span></p>
<table id="S6.E45" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.E45X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E45X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E45Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E45Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E45Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}+\dfrac{1}{T^{\frac{3}{2}}}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}+\dfrac{1}{T^{\frac{3}{2}}}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E45Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E45Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">To achieve the <math id="Thmcorollary1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> rate, it suffices to have</span></p>
<table id="S6.E46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S6.EGx17" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E46.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E46.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\right)\lesssim T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\right)\lesssim T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E46.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E46.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]\right)\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]\right)\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="Thmcorollary1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy (<a href="#S6.E43" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a>) for <math id="Thmcorollary1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>, then (<a href="#S6.E46" title="In Proof. ‣ Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>) holds, yielding <math id="Thmcorollary1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> rate.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now suppose Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 (PL condition). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> also holds. Setting <math id="Thmcorollary1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-1}\ln T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-1}\ln T)</annotation></semantics></math> and substituting (<a href="#S6.E44" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>) into (<a href="#S5.E38" title="In Theorem 5. ‣ 5.2 Convergence with PL Condition ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="S6.E47" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.E47X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E47X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{1}{T}+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{1}{T}+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E47Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E47Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E47Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{T-t}\hskip-2.84526pt\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{T-t}\hskip-2.84526pt\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E47Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E47Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E47Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\hskip-1.42262pt\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\left(\hskip-2.84526pt1\hskip-1.42262pt-\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\hskip-1.42262pt\right)^{\hskip-2.84526ptT-t}\hskip-4.2679pt\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=2}^{N}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt\right)\hskip-2.84526pt." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\hskip-1.42262pt\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\left(\hskip-2.84526pt1\hskip-1.42262pt-\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\hskip-1.42262pt\right)^{\hskip-2.84526ptT-t}\hskip-4.2679pt\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=2}^{N}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\hskip-1.42262pt\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt\right)\hskip-2.84526pt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">To achieve the <math id="Thmcorollary1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> rate, it suffices to have</span></p>
<table id="S6.E48" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S6.EGx18" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E48.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E48.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\lesssim 1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\lesssim 1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E48.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E48.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\right\|^{2}\right]+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">where we have used the fact that</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{T-t}\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{T-t}\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="Thmcorollary1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy (<a href="#S6.E44" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>) for <math id="Thmcorollary1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>, then (<a href="#S6.E48" title="In Proof. ‣ Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) holds, yielding <math id="Thmcorollary1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> rate.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> quantifies admissible magnitudes of <em id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">frequent</em> perturbations along the forward and backward passes.
From (<a href="#S6.E43" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a>) and (<a href="#S6.E44" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>), one sees that the conditional bias terms
<math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]\|</annotation></semantics></math>
are subject to stricter requirements than the corresponding second-moment terms
<math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\|</annotation></semantics></math>.
More importantly, the forward perturbations <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy stronger conditions than the backward perturbations <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>
to retain the error-free rate.
For example, when both perturbations are unbiased (i.e., <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}_{t}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>), SGD on nonconvex objectives can still achieve <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
but generally fails to do so with <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This forward/backward asymmetry will be further reflected in the remark below.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Inequality (<a href="#S6.E45" title="In Proof. ‣ Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>) suggests decomposing the bound into two components: an optimization error that vanishes with increased optimization effort (smaller <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and/or larger <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>), and a perturbation-induced perturbation error. As noted in Remark <a href="#Thmremark3" title="Remark 3. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, under frequent perturbations, forward errors constitute the primary obstruction to eliminating this perturbation error: unless the forward perturbations satisfy the stricter decay conditions, the bound predicts a non-vanishing plateau even as <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. By contrast, in the unbiased regime, backward perturbations are comparatively benign and contribute primarily to the optimization error. In Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we illustrate this distinction explicit through two parameter sweeps: a coupled sweep with <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1/\sqrt{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1/\sqrt{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a fixed-horizon sweep with common <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and varying <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we consider the convergence of SGD under intermittent perturbations. For each time step <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and component <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\cdots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\cdots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that an intermittent perturbation occurs in the forward pass if <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is of order <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, for the backward pass, an intermittent perturbation occurs if <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is of order <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the total number of perturbation occurrences throughout optimization in the forward and backward passes, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following two corollaries establish upper bounds on the number of admissible intermittent perturbation occurrences that preserve the convergence rate, under the assumption that the perturbations are zero-mean.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose the perturbations <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> are zero-mean for all <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Smoothness of the loss function). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, SGD achieves a convergence rate of <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> provided that <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting <math id="Thmcorollary2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmcorollary2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#S5.E37" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>), and using the definitions of <math id="Thmcorollary2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S6.E49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.E49X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E49X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E49Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E49Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E49Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{1}{T^{\frac{3}{2}}}\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hskip-2.84526pt\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=2}^{N}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-1.42262pt\right)\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{1}{T}\hskip-1.42262pt\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{1}{T^{\frac{3}{2}}}\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hskip-2.84526pt\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=2}^{N}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-1.42262pt\right)\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{1}{T}\hskip-1.42262pt\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E49Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E49Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E49Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}+\dfrac{Q_{\delta}+Q_{\varepsilon}}{T^{\frac{3}{2}}}+\dfrac{Q_{\delta}}{T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}+\dfrac{Q_{\delta}+Q_{\varepsilon}}{T^{\frac{3}{2}}}+\dfrac{Q_{\delta}}{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, if <math id="Thmcorollary2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]\lesssim T^{-\frac{1}{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose the perturbations <math id="Thmcorollary3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> are zero-mean for all <math id="Thmcorollary3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Smoothness of the loss function). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 (PL condition). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, SGD achieves a convergence rate of <math id="Thmcorollary3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> provided that <math id="Thmcorollary3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\lesssim 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\lesssim 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcorollary3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Under the PL condition, substituting <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{G}_{i}}_{t}[\delta_{i}^{(t)}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i}}[\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-1}\ln T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-1}\ln T)</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#S5.E37" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>), and using the definitions of <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex3X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})\hskip-1.42262pt-\hskip-1.42262pt\ell^{*}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})\hskip-1.42262pt-\hskip-1.42262pt\ell^{*}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\hskip-1.42262pt\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\left(1\hskip-1.42262pt-\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{\hskip-2.84526ptT-t}\hskip-2.84526pt\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-1.42262pt\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\hskip-1.42262pt\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hskip-1.42262pt\left(1\hskip-1.42262pt-\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{\hskip-2.84526ptT-t}\hskip-2.84526pt\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-2.84526pt+\hskip-2.84526pt\sum_{i=2}^{N}\mathbb{E}\hskip-1.42262pt\left[\left\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{2}\right]\hskip-1.42262pt\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex3Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{T-t}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.167em">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{T-t}\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\right\|^{4}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex3Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex3Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex3Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}+\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\sum_{t=1}^{\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{t}+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{Q_{\delta}}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}+\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\sum_{t=1}^{\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{t}+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{Q_{\delta}}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Evaluating the geometric sums yields</span></p>
<table id="S6.E50" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.E50X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E50X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E50X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}+\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\cdot\dfrac{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)^{\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}}}{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}+\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\cdot\dfrac{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)^{\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}}}{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E50Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E50Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\cdot\dfrac{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)^{Q_{\delta}}}{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\cdot\dfrac{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)^{Q_{\delta}}}{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E50Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E50Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E50Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}(1+Q_{\delta})+\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\max\{Q_{\delta}+Q_{\varepsilon}\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}(1+Q_{\delta})+\dfrac{\ln^{2}T}{T^{2}}\max\{Q_{\delta}+Q_{\varepsilon}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary3.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the last inequality follows from the fact that <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\ln T/T)^{\alpha}\geq 1-\alpha\ln T/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\ln T/T)^{\alpha}\geq 1-\alpha\ln T/T</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, if <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\lesssim 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\lesssim 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following corollary establishes upper bounds on the number of admissible intermittent perturbation occurrences <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> the zero-mean assumption, under nonconvex and PL settings respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Smoothness of the loss function). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Operator smoothness). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, SGD achieves a convergence rate of <math id="Thmcorollary4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmcorollary4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, if Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 (PL condition). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> also holds, then SGD achieves a convergence rate of <math id="Thmcorollary4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(T^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmcorollary4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcorollary4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting <math id="Thmcorollary4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#S5.E37" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>) and using the definitions of <math id="Thmcorollary4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S6.E51" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.E51X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E51X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}+\dfrac{Q_{\delta}+Q_{\varepsilon}}{T^{\frac{3}{2}}}+\dfrac{Q_{\delta}+Q_{\varepsilon}}{T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}+\dfrac{Q_{\delta}+Q_{\varepsilon}}{T^{\frac{3}{2}}}+\dfrac{Q_{\delta}+Q_{\varepsilon}}{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, if <math id="Thmcorollary4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim T^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\nabla\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(t)})\right\|^{2}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcorollary4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now suppose Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 (PL condition). ‣ 3.3 Assumptions ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> also holds. Substituting <math id="Thmcorollary4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-1}\ln T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\mathcal{O}(T^{-1}\ln T)</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#S5.E38" title="In Theorem 5. ‣ 5.2 Convergence with PL Condition ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) and using the definitions of <math id="Thmcorollary4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S6.E52" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.E52X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E52X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E52X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}+\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}}\left(1-\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)^{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.E52Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E52Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E52Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\hskip-2.84526pt\left(\hskip-2.84526pt1\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)^{\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}}}{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)}\hskip-2.84526pt\right)\hskip-2.84526pt\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T}(\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt1)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\hskip-2.84526pt\left(\hskip-2.84526pt1\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt\dfrac{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)^{\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}}}{1-\left(1-{\ln T}/{T}\right)}\hskip-2.84526pt\right)\hskip-2.84526pt\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T}(\max\{Q_{\delta},Q_{\varepsilon}\}\hskip-1.42262pt+\hskip-1.42262pt1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, if <math id="Thmcorollary4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\lesssim 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\lesssim 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}\lesssim 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\boldsymbol{w}^{(T)})-\ell^{*}\right]\lesssim\dfrac{\ln T}{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary4.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary4.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">which completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Upper bounds on the number of <math id="S6.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>-magnitude intermittent perturbation occurrences in the forward and backward passes that SGD can tolerate without deteriorating the convergence rate. “Zero mean” indicates that the intermittent
perturbations have zero expectation.</figcaption>
<table id="S6.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S6.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">Assumption</td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">Convergence rate</td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">Zero mean</td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Non-convex</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S6.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S6.T1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S6.T1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T^{\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T^{\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">PL condition</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S6.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\dfrac{\ln T}{T}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S6.T1.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S6.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S6.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the admissible occurrence budgets of intermittent perturbations under different assumptions,
as derived from Corollaries <a href="#Thmcorollary2" title="Corollary 2. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#Thmcorollary3" title="Corollary 3. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and <a href="#Thmcorollary4" title="Corollary 4. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Without the zero-mean assumption, the admissible occurrence thresholds for forward and backward perturbations coincide under both the nonconvex and PL settings.
In contrast, under zero-mean perturbations—i.e., when each perturbed forward activation and backward chain computation is (conditionally) unbiased—a pronounced asymmetry emerges:
the admissible occurrence frequency of backward perturbations can be relaxed to <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that unbiased backward perturbations may persist throughout optimization without changing the convergence rate order.
For forward perturbations, however, the maximum allowable occurrence frequencies that preserve convergence are much smaller: <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{1/2})</annotation></semantics></math> in the nonconvex setting and <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> under the PL condition; exceeding these thresholds may lead to slower convergence or divergence (see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Non-convergence Induced by Frequent 𝒪(1) Magnitude Perturbations in Forward Propagation ‣ Appendix A Counter examples ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> for a one-dimensional example where persistent <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> forward perturbations cause convergence to a biased limit point).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5</span></span><span id="Thmremark5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the intermittent regime, the admissible occurrence-budget bounds in Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are most naturally expressed in terms of the total perturbation counts <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q_{\delta},Q_{\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q_{\delta},Q_{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> over the horizon <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, if a forward perturbation is injected once every <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> steps, then <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\approx\lceil T/\Delta t\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\approx\lceil T/\Delta t\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> (and analogously for <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>), so varying <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> directly controls the effective perturbation budget. The asymmetry highlighted in Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can therefore be recast as an asymmetric budget requirement: in the unbiased setting, forward perturbations of order <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> must be sufficiently infrequent (i.e., <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> must remain small), whereas backward perturbations may occur considerably more often (i.e., <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> may be substantially larger). This budget-centric perspective foreshadows the phase-transition behavior observed in Section <a href="#S7.SS2.SSS3" title="7.2.3 Convergence with intermittent forward computation perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2.3</span></a> as <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> increases.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Convergence with Gradient Spikes</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we characterize conditions under which the optimization process still converges in the presence of gradient spikes. We say that the evaluated gradient at iteration <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{u}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{u}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, exhibits a <em id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gradient spike</em> if its deviation from the (unspiked) stochastic gradient evaluation satisfies</p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\|\tilde{\boldsymbol{u}}^{(t)}-\boldsymbol{u}^{(t)}\bigr\|^{2}=\mathcal{O}(1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\|\tilde{\boldsymbol{u}}^{(t)}-\boldsymbol{u}^{(t)}\bigr\|^{2}=\mathcal{O}(1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In Theorem 2. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>), this occurs whenever there exists some <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,\ldots,N-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,\ldots,N-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that either <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta_{i}^{(t)}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\varepsilon_{i}^{(t)}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> directly implies how frequently gradient spikes may occur before they begin to slow down the convergence rate under the different assumptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present numerical experiments to validate our theoretical
findings. Specifically, we consider the logistic regression problem:</p>
<table id="S7.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{w\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\ f(w):=\dfrac{1}{M}\sum_{l=1}^{M}f_{2}(w;(h_{l},y_{l}))+\rho\mathcal{R}(w)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.667em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{w\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\ f(w):=\dfrac{1}{M}\sum_{l=1}^{M}f_{2}(w;(h_{l},y_{l}))+\rho\mathcal{R}(w).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(h_{l},y_{l})\}_{l=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(h_{l},y_{l})\}_{l=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the training samples, where <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the feature vector and <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}\in\{+1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}\in\{+1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding label. The logistic loss <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}(w;(h_{l},y_{l}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}(w;(h_{l},y_{l}))</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed in the form of (<a href="#S1.E1.2" title="In 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1b</span></a>) as:</p>
<table id="S7.E54" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S7.E54X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E54X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{1}(w;(h_{l},y_{l})):=-y_{l}h_{l}^{\top}w,\quad f_{2}(w;(h_{l},y_{l})):=\ln(1+\exp[f_{1}(w;(h_{l},y_{l}))])." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{1}(w;(h_{l},y_{l})):=-y_{l}h_{l}^{\top}w,\quad f_{2}(w;(h_{l},y_{l})):=\ln(1+\exp[f_{1}(w;(h_{l},y_{l}))]).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The term <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> is a regularizer and <math id="S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a given constant.
We consider both a non-convex and a strongly convex choice of <math id="S7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the non-convex case, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, we set <math id="S7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(w):=\sum_{j=1}^{d}{[w]_{j}^{2}}/{(1+[w]_{j}^{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(w):=\sum_{j=1}^{d}{[w]_{j}^{2}}/{(1+[w]_{j}^{2})}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[w]_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[w]_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of <math id="S7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. In the strongly convex case,
we set <math id="S7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(w)=\|w\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(w)=\|w\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/nonconvex_unbiased_forward_norm_show_new1.svg" id="S7.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/nonconvex_unbiased_backward_norm_show_new1.svg" id="S7.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The convergence performance with forward and backward computation error with different step size <math id="S7.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for the logistic regression task with non-convex regularization. (Left: <math id="S7.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}=0.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: <math id="S7.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=0.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S7.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/nonconvex_unbiased_forward_norm_new1.svg" id="S7.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F4.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/nonconvex_unbiased_backward_norm_new1.svg" id="S7.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The relationship between the stable gradient norm and the step size <math id="S7.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with forward and backward computation error for the logistic regression task with non-convex regularization. (Left: Varied <math id="S7.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: Varied <math id="S7.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We set <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=2000</annotation></semantics></math>, and use a fixed horizon <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=200{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=200{,}000</annotation></semantics></math>. To generate the data samples, we first draw <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\star}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\star}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and feature vectors <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding label <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by drawing <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{l}\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{l}\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and setting <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{l}\leq 1/(1+\exp(-h_{l}^{\top}x^{\star}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{l}\leq 1/(1+\exp(-h_{l}^{\top}x^{\star}))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}=-1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. At each gradient evaluation, we inject perturbations after computing the forward activation <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (forward perturbation <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) and after computing the backward chain term <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial f_{2}/\partial f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial f_{2}/\partial f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (backward perturbation <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>). Let <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\nabla f}(w_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\nabla f}(w_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the resulting perturbed gradient. To emulate SGD with controlled variance, we form the stochastic gradient as</p>
<table id="S7.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}\;=\;\widetilde{\nabla f}(w_{t})\;+\;\xi_{t},\quad\xi_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{n}^{2}I_{d}),\quad\sigma_{n}=0.001," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}\;=\;\widetilde{\nabla f}(w_{t})\;+\;\xi_{t},\quad\xi_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{n}^{2}I_{d}),\quad\sigma_{n}=0.001,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote forward and backward computation perturbations, respectively, while <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes sampling noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the effect of these perturbations on training, we use the gradient norm <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla f(w_{t})\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla f(w_{t})\|</annotation></semantics></math> as the primary evaluation metric. We smooth its trajectory via an exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>) and define two derived quantities: the <em id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stable gradient norm</em>, the average EWMA value over the last <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor T/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor T/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> iterations; and the <em id="S7.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stable iteration complexity</em>, the first iteration at which the EWMA falls below <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> the stable gradient norm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As a baseline, we consider standard SGD with no perturbations in either pass (i.e., <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{t}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{t}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>), reducing the update to sampling noise only:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}=\nabla f(w_{t})+\xi_{t},\qquad w_{t+1}=w_{t}-\gamma g_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}=\nabla f(w_{t})+\xi_{t},\qquad w_{t+1}=w_{t}-\gamma g_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This baseline appears as “Standard SGD” in the legend of plots where only one perturbation type is varied.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In what follows, we report numerical results for three perturbation regimes (frequent zero-mean perturbations,
frequent non-zero-mean perturbations, and intermittent perturbations), and focus on a single observable throughout: whether the stable gradient norm continues to decrease toward the standard-SGD baseline as the step size shrinks, or instead saturates at a step-size-insensitive plateau. This behavior directly reflects the qualitative predictions of Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> for frequent perturbations (e.g., Remark <a href="#Thmremark4" title="Remark 4. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and Section <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> for intermittent perturbations parameterized by occurrence budgets (e.g., Remark <a href="#Thmremark5" title="Remark 5. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Specifically, Section <a href="#S7.SS2.SSS1" title="7.2.1 Convergence with frequent zero-mean perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2.1</span></a> considers frequent zero-mean perturbations and highlights the asymmetry between forward and backward perturbations; Section <a href="#S7.SS2.SSS2" title="7.2.2 Convergence with frequent non-zero-mean computation perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2.2</span></a> isolates persistent non-zero-mean bias, which manifests as an irreducible plateau that cannot be eliminated by shrinking the step size alone; Section <a href="#S7.SS2.SSS3" title="7.2.3 Convergence with intermittent forward computation perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2.3</span></a> studies intermittent perturbations, where varying the injection interval controls the effective occurrence budget and produces a clear phase transition.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/convex_unbiased_forward_norm_new1.svg" id="S7.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F5.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/convex_unbiased_backward_norm_new1.svg" id="S7.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The relationship between the stable gradient norm and the step size <math id="S7.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with forward and backward computation perturbations for the logistic regression task with strongly convex (hence PL) regularization. (Left: Varied <math id="S7.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: Varied <math id="S7.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.2.1 </span>Convergence with frequent zero-mean perturbations</h4>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate convergence under zero-mean forward and backward perturbations, we inject symmetric noise <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in the forward pass and <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in the backward pass, drawn independently and uniformly as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta\sim\mathcal{U}(-\sigma_{f}/\sqrt{d},\,\sigma_{f}/\sqrt{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\sim\mathcal{U}(-\sigma_{f}/\sqrt{d},\,\sigma_{f}/\sqrt{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varepsilon\sim\mathcal{U}(-\sigma_{b}/\sqrt{d},\,\sigma_{b}/\sqrt{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\sim\mathcal{U}(-\sigma_{b}/\sqrt{d},\,\sigma_{b}/\sqrt{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> control the respective perturbation magnitudes. The regularization coefficient is set to <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.001</annotation></semantics></math> in the nonconvex case and <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> in the strongly convex case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the nonconvex setting (Figures <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), the standard-SGD baseline exhibits the expected behavior: the stable gradient norm decreases and tends to <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Under zero-mean backward perturbations, the same qualitative behavior persists: Figure <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (right) shows smaller stabilized gradient norms for smaller step sizes, and Figure <a href="#S7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (right) confirms that the stable gradient norm continues to decrease toward <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> across all tested <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> values.
This is consistent with Remark <a href="#Thmremark4" title="Remark 4. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (see also Remark <a href="#Thmremark3" title="Remark 3. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>): in the frequent unbiased regime, backward perturbations are comparatively benign and contribute primarily to the optimization error, so shrinking <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> reduces the stable gradient norm without inducing a step-size-insensitive plateau.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For zero-mean forward perturbations, however, the behavior depends on the perturbation magnitude. Figure <a href="#S7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (left) indicates that for small perturbation levels the stable gradient norm still decreases with <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, but once the forward perturbation magnitude becomes non-negligible (e.g., <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}\geq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}\geq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>), the stable gradient norm saturates at a nonzero plateau even as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. This plateau is further corroborated by the trajectories in Figure <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (left) under <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, where shrinking <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> yields little improvement. This behavior illustrates the forward/backward asymmetry in Remark <a href="#Thmremark4" title="Remark 4. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (see also  Remark <a href="#Thmremark3" title="Remark 3. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>): unless the forward perturbations satisfy the stricter decay conditions in Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the bound predicts a non-vanishing plateau even as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the strongly convex (hence PL) setting (Figure <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), the same qualitative picture holds: the stable gradient norm under backward perturbations can still be driven toward <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas sufficiently large forward perturbations pin it at a nonzero plateau regardless of the step size. This is again consistent with Remark <a href="#Thmremark4" title="Remark 4. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> together with the PL part of Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: unbiased backward perturbations do not prevent the stable gradient norm from decreasing with <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas sufficiently large forward perturbations pin it at a nonzero, step-size-insensitive plateau.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, Figures <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>–<a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> provide a direct illustration of Remark <a href="#Thmremark4" title="Remark 4. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>: decreasing the step size mainly reduces the optimization error component, whereas the perturbation-induced component manifests as a step-size-insensitive plateau when forward perturbations are too large. In line with Remark <a href="#Thmremark3" title="Remark 3. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the forward perturbations must satisfy stricter control conditions than the backward perturbations in order to avoid this plateau and recover the standard-SGD trend as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.2.2 </span>Convergence with frequent non-zero-mean computation perturbations</h4>

<figure id="S7.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:60%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/nonconvex_biased_backward_norm_new1.svg" id="S7.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:267/200;" width="267" height="200"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>The relationship between the stable gradient norm and the step size <math id="S7.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with non-zero-mean backward computation perturbations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To isolate the effect of a persistent mean bias, we inject a non-zero-mean perturbation at every iteration in the <em id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backward</em> computation while keeping the forward pass unperturbed: <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\sim\mathcal{U}(0,\sigma_{b}/\sqrt{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\sim\mathcal{U}(0,\sigma_{b}/\sqrt{d})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S7.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 7.2.2 Convergence with frequent non-zero-mean computation perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a clear qualitative difference from the zero-mean case:
even as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the stable gradient norm no longer tends to <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> once <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and instead saturates at a nonzero plateau that grows with <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The curve corresponding to zero perturbation magnitude (i.e., no injected computation perturbations) recovers the standard-SGD behavior, with the stable gradient norm decreasing toward <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This is consistent with the conditional-bias requirements in Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (see Remark <a href="#Thmremark3" title="Remark 3. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and the bias characterization in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: once <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i+1}}[\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}]\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbb{E}_{t}^{\mathcal{H}_{i+1}}[\varepsilon_{i+1}^{(t)}]\|</annotation></semantics></math> is persistently nonzero (here induced by the non-zero-mean sampling), the theory contains an irreducible bias-driven term, which manifests empirically as a non-vanishing plateau that cannot be removed by shrinking <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> alone.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.2.3 </span>Convergence with intermittent forward computation perturbations</h4>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate convergence properties under intermittent perturbations in forward propagation, we introduce zero-mean noise <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta\sim\mathcal{U}(-\sigma_{f}/\sqrt{d},\sigma_{f}/\sqrt{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\sim\mathcal{U}(-\sigma_{f}/\sqrt{d},\sigma_{f}/\sqrt{d})</annotation></semantics></math> during forward propagation at intervals of <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations in logistic regression with both non-convex and convex regularization terms. The parameter <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> governs the noise injection frequency. The regularization coefficient <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is configured as 0.001 for non-convex scenarios and 0.5 for convex cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#S7.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 7.2.3 Convergence with intermittent forward computation perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#S7.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 7.2.3 Convergence with intermittent forward computation perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> report the stable gradient norm versus the step size <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> under intermittent forward perturbations with injection interval <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>;
these correspond to the nonconvex and PL intermittent regimes analyzed in Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary2" title="Corollary 2. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary3" title="Corollary 3. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, respectively.
The curve labeled “Standard SGD” corresponds to standard SGD with no injected computation perturbations, and its stable gradient norm decreases toward <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is small, the stable gradient norm exhibits a nonzero plateau even as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, resembling the frequent-perturbation regime. As <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> increases and spikes become rarer, the plateau height decreases and the curves progressively recover the error-free behavior, with the stable gradient norm again tending to <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, for larger forward perturbation magnitudes, recovery requires a larger <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with the fact that stronger spikes demand stricter sparsity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Injecting a forward perturbation once every <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations implies that the number of forward spike iterations scales as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}\approx\lceil T/\Delta t_{f}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}\approx\lceil T/\Delta t_{f}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> over a horizon <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, sweeping <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> directly tunes the effective occurrence budget <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in Section 6.2 and Table 1.
In particular, Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary2" title="Corollary 2. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (nonconvex) and Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary3" title="Corollary 3. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (PL) specify the admissible order of <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>) that preserves the error-free rate under zero-mean intermittent spikes,
and Remark <a href="#Thmremark5" title="Remark 5. ‣ 6.2 Convergence Conditions for Intermittent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> interprets the resulting thresholds as a budget requirement; the progressive recovery in Figures <a href="#S7.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 7.2.3 Convergence with intermittent forward computation perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>–<a href="#S7.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 7.2.3 Convergence with intermittent forward computation perturbations ‣ 7.2 Experimental Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> as <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> increases is consistent with this budget-threshold picture.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F7.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/nonconvex_intermittent_forward_norm1_new1.svg" id="S7.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F7.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/nonconvex_intermittent_forward_norm_new1.svg" id="S7.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>The relationship between the stable gradient norm and the step size <math id="S7.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with intermittent forward perturbations for the logistic regression task in the non-convex scenario. (Left: <math id="S7.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: <math id="S7.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S7.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F8.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/convex_intermittent_forward_norm1_new1.svg" id="S7.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F8.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/convex_intermittent_forward_norm_new1.svg" id="S7.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>The relationship between the stable gradient norm and the step size <math id="S7.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with intermittent forward perturbations for the logistic regression task in the strongly convex (hence PL) scenario. (Left: <math id="S7.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: <math id="S7.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>.)</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion and Future Works</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper studies the effect of computational perturbations on gradient propagation in stochastic gradient descent, with particular emphasis on their accumulation across successive operators. Through rigorous analysis of error dynamics in both the forward and backward passes, we establish convergence guarantees for SGD under general non-convex objectives and the Polyak–Łojasiewicz condition in the presence of persistent computational inaccuracies. Our theoretical framework characterizes the admissible magnitudes for continuous perturbations and the tolerable frequencies for intermittent perturbations required to preserve standard convergence rates. Experimental results provide empirical validation of our theoretical propositions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Future research directions encompass several key aspects. First, it remains to be seen whether simple correctors can restore error-free SGD rates under frequent <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> forward perturbations, for example through delta-based activation compression combined with error-compensation schemes that render accumulated distortion summable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Second, explicit forward-pass de-biasing techniques, may eliminate the nonlinearity-induced bias terms underlying Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4 Error Propagation Analysis ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Finally, extending the theory to deep and realistic mixed-precision or quantized pipelines, where perturbations are state-dependent and interact with momentum or adaptive stepsizes, presents a significant challenge.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
