<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:144%;">Appendix<span id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Counter examples</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present some counter examples that has not discussed in the previous sections.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Zero-mean Noise can Lead to Biased Evaluated Gradient</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here is a straightforward example that illustrates the theoretical challenge: even perturbations with zero expectation can introduce bias in the evaluated gradient.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1</span></span><span id="Thmexample1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Take the Sigmiod function <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(y)=\sigma(y):=(1+e^{-y})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(y)=\sigma(y):=(1+e^{-y})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose derivative given by <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(y)=\sigma(y)(1-\sigma(y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(y)=\sigma(y)(1-\sigma(y))</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric and reaches its peak when <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math>. If we assume <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}^{(t)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}^{(t)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a symmetric two-point distribution on <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{-a,a\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{-a,a\}</annotation></semantics></math> with equal masses. Consequently, <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}]=0=y_{i-1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}]=0=y_{i-1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is an unbiased estimate of <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y}_{i-1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We find that <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i-1}^{(t)})=\sigma^{\prime}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i-1}^{(t)})=\sigma^{\prime}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. The expected value of the derivative calculates as:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[f_{i}^{\prime}(\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[f_{i}^{\prime}(\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{\prime}(a)+\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{\prime}(-a)=\sigma^{\prime}(a)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{\prime}(a)+\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{\prime}(-a)=\sigma^{\prime}(a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(a)&lt;\sigma^{\prime}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(a)&lt;\sigma^{\prime}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[f_{i}^{\prime}(\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)})]&lt;f_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i-1}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[f_{i}^{\prime}(\widetilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)})]&lt;f_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i-1}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the relevant gradient component of <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (like a partial derivative concerning the first variable), this inequality implies:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\nabla f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})]\neq\nabla f_{i}({y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\nabla f_{i}(\tilde{y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)})]\neq\nabla f_{i}({y}_{i-1}^{(t)},w_{i}^{(t)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This example highlights a key asymmetry between forward and backward perturbations: even when the injected noise is mean-zero at the level of intermediate variables, the resulting gradient estimate can become biased due to nonlinearity of the computational graph. This phenomenon motivates the higher-moment bias control terms (e.g., the <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\|\delta\|^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\|\delta\|^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> contributions), and explains why the forward perturbation thresholds in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> are typically more stringent than their backward counterparts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Non-convergence Induced by Frequent <math id="A1.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> Magnitude Perturbations in Forward Propagation</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first construct a counterexample under gradient descent algorithm to demonstrate non-convergence caused by persistent <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> magnitude perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the deterministic gradient descent (GD) algorithm with objective function defined for all <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(x)=x^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(x)=x^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">At iteration <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward propagation introduces an additive perturbation term yielding <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}(x^{(t)})=(x^{(t)}+\delta)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}(x^{(t)})=(x^{(t)}+\delta)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a fixed perturbation constant. The corrupted gradient computation becomes <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}^{(t)}:=2(x^{(t)}+\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}^{(t)}:=2(x^{(t)}+\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. And this leads to the iteration scheme:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x^{(t+1)}=x^{(t)}-\gamma\boldsymbol{u}^{(t)}=x^{(t)}-2\gamma(x^{(t)}+\delta)=(1-2\gamma)x^{(t)}-2\gamma\delta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x^{(t+1)}=x^{(t)}-\gamma\boldsymbol{u}^{(t)}=x^{(t)}-2\gamma(x^{(t)}+\delta)=(1-2\gamma)x^{(t)}-2\gamma\delta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the learning rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&lt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&lt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{(t)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{(t)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating systematic deviation from the true minimum at zero. This phenomenon underscores the inherent instability of gradient-based methods when subjected to persistent <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> forward propagation perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This example shows that persistent <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>-magnitude forward perturbations can shift the effective optimization dynamics, causing SGD to converge to a biased limit point (or, more generally, to a neighborhood that does not shrink with <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>). This concretely illustrates the “error-floor” interpretation of the bias-type terms in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 5.1 Convergence with Non-Convex Assumption ‣ 5 Convergence of SGD with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and the necessity of the decay/sparsity conditions stated in Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 6.1 Convergence Conditions for Frequent Perturbation ‣ 6 Conditions Ensuring Convergence under Perturbations ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Non-convergence With Top-K Compressor</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a counter example to illustrate the non-convergence of SGD algorithm with standard non-convex assumption under Top-K compression.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we denote the sample and parameter vectors as:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x=\left(x_{1},x_{2}\right)^{\top},\quad w_{1}=\left(w_{11},w_{12}\right)^{\top},\quad w_{2}=\left(w_{21},w_{22}\right)^{\top},\quad A=\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\end{pmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x=\left(x_{1},x_{2}\right)^{\top},\quad w_{1}=\left(w_{11},w_{12}\right)^{\top},\quad w_{2}=\left(w_{21},w_{22}\right)^{\top},\quad A=\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then, we take the objective function <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{w}):=f_{2}(f_{1}(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{w}_{1}),\boldsymbol{w}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{w}):=f_{2}(f_{1}(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{w}_{1}),\boldsymbol{w}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the function <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{1}(x,w_{1})=A(x\odot w_{1})=\begin{pmatrix}ax_{1}w_{11}+bx_{2}w_{12}\\
cx_{1}w_{11}+dx_{2}w_{12}\end{pmatrix},\quad f_{2}(y_{1},w_{2})=\langle y_{1},w_{2}\rangle^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\|y_{1}\|^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{1}(x,w_{1})=A(x\odot w_{1})=\begin{pmatrix}ax_{1}w_{11}+bx_{2}w_{12}\\
cx_{1}w_{11}+dx_{2}w_{12}\end{pmatrix},\quad f_{2}(y_{1},w_{2})=\langle y_{1},w_{2}\rangle^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\|y_{1}\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we introduce the Top-1 compression operator <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, which select the element with the maximum absolute value and set all the other elements to zero. With <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradients of <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed as follows:</p>
<table id="A1.E56" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A1.E56X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E56X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E56X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\nabla_{w_{2}}f_{2}(y_{1},w_{2})=2\langle{y}_{1},w_{2}\rangle{y}_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\nabla_{w_{2}}f_{2}(y_{1},w_{2})=2\langle{y}_{1},w_{2}\rangle{y}_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E56Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E56Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{v}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{v}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E56Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\nabla_{{y}_{1}}f_{2}({y}_{1},w_{2})=2\langle{y}_{1},w_{2}\rangle{w}_{2}+{y}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\nabla_{{y}_{1}}f_{2}({y}_{1},w_{2})=2\langle{y}_{1},w_{2}\rangle{w}_{2}+{y}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E56Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E56Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{u}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{u}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E56Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\left(\nabla_{{w}_{1}}f_{1}({x},{w}_{1})\right)^{\top}{\mathcal{C}_{G}}\left({v}_{1}\right)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}ax_{1}&amp;bx_{2}\\
cx_{1}&amp;dx_{2}\end{array}\right)^{\top}{\mathcal{C}_{G}}\left(2\langle{y}_{1},{w}_{2}\rangle{w}_{2}+{y}_{1}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\left(\nabla_{{w}_{1}}f_{1}({x},{w}_{1})\right)^{\top}{\mathcal{C}_{G}}\left({v}_{1}\right)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}ax_{1}&amp;bx_{2}\\
cx_{1}&amp;dx_{2}\end{array}\right)^{\top}{\mathcal{C}_{G}}\left(2\langle{y}_{1},{w}_{2}\rangle{w}_{2}+{y}_{1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Top-1 compression operator.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F9.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/top1_nonconvergence_0.1_0.0.svg" id="A1.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/171;" width="229" height="171"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F9.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20646v1/top1_nonconvergence_0.05_0.9.svg" id="A1.F9.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/171;" width="229" height="171"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>The norm of the parameter <math id="A1.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by SGD with Top-1 compressor and the comparison with the non-compression case. Note that the Top-1 cases all fail to converge. (Left: <math id="A1.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: <math id="A1.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Set the initial values <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{1}^{(1)}=\left(s,s\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1}^{(1)}=\left(s,s\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{2}^{(1)}=\left(0,0\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{2}^{(1)}=\left(0,0\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, and fix <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x}^{(k)}\equiv x=\left(1,1\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x}^{(k)}\equiv x=\left(1,1\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,3,\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,3,\cdots</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is a given coefficient. Then we can derive the recurrence relations for the <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration as follows:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex15X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{y}_{1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{y}_{1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\end{array}\right){w}_{1}^{(t)},\quad{v}_{1}^{(t)}={\mathcal{C}_{G}}\left({y}_{1}^{(t)}\right),\quad{u}_{1}^{(t)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\end{array}\right)^{\top}{v}_{1}^{(t)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\end{array}\right){w}_{1}^{(t)},\quad{v}_{1}^{(t)}={\mathcal{C}_{G}}\left({y}_{1}^{(t)}\right),\quad{u}_{1}^{(t)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\end{array}\right)^{\top}{v}_{1}^{(t)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex15Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{w}_{1}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{w}_{1}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={w}_{1}^{(t)}-\gamma{u}_{1}^{(t)},\quad{u}_{2}^{(t)}={0},\quad{w}_{2}^{(t)}\equiv\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≡</mo><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={w}_{1}^{(t)}-\gamma{u}_{1}^{(t)},\quad{u}_{2}^{(t)}={0},\quad{w}_{2}^{(t)}\equiv\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">If we take <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=-15,b=13,c=-5,d=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=-15,b=13,c=-5,d=9</annotation></semantics></math> and set the learning rate <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, our counterexample converges to <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{1}={0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1}={0}</annotation></semantics></math> without compression (i.e., when <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity operator). Specifically, with no computation perturbation, we have:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.EGx31.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.EGx31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{y}_{1}^{(1)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}-2s\\
4s\end{array}\right),\quad{v}_{1}^{(1)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}-2s\\
4s\end{array}\right),\quad{u}_{1}^{(1)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}10s\\
10s\end{array}\right),\quad{w}_{1}^{(2)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{y}_{1}^{(1)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}-2s\\
4s\end{array}\right),\quad{v}_{1}^{(1)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}-2s\\
4s\end{array}\right),\quad{u}_{1}^{(1)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}10s\\
10s\end{array}\right),\quad{w}_{1}^{(2)}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With momentum SGD, the updating rule of <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{1}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{2}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{2}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{1}^{(t)}={w}_{1}^{(t-1)}-\eta{u}_{1}^{(t)}+\beta\left({w}_{1}^{(t-1)}-{w}_{1}^{(t-2)}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1}^{(t)}={w}_{1}^{(t-1)}-\eta{u}_{1}^{(t)}+\beta\left({w}_{1}^{(t-1)}-{w}_{1}^{(t-2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, when we take <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=-5,b=-4,c=-5,d=3,\gamma=0.05,\beta=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=-5,b=-4,c=-5,d=3,\gamma=0.05,\beta=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, numerical study implies that the momentum SGD without the compression error achieve the convergence as Figure <a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ A.3 Non-convergence With Top-K Compressor ‣ Appendix A Counter examples ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">However, Figure <a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ A.3 Non-convergence With Top-K Compressor ‣ Appendix A Counter examples ‣ On the Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with Perturbed Forward-Backward Passes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> also illustrates that the parameter cannot convergence with the Top-1 compression with the same setup as the no-compression cases in both SGD optimization and momentum SGD. This result validates our theoretical finding that it cannot converge under frequent perturbation with <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> magnitude.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
